Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 16:14:18 EST
From: pmetzger@snark.shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: PHIL/META: A Challenge to be Rational

> From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
> 
> I seem to recall mention here of an Extropian ritual involving the ashes of
> a dollar bill being smeared on each other's foreheads.  Would any of the
> participants in that event care to comment on its purpose and rationality?

Purpose: It was amusing.
Rationality: Since it amused us, and our goal was amusement, and it had
no significant adverse consequences, it was rational to do it.

As an example of something similar, I'm a member of a collegiate literary
and debate society at Columbia University called "The Philolexian Society".
(I graduated a million years ago, but once a Philo always a Philo -- you
are tainted for life.) We used to have these long meetings in which we
would follow the rules of parliamentary proceedure excruciatingly while
debating resolutions such as "Resolved: If you have to ask, you'll never
know". Afterwards, we used to go to the old Cathedral Cafe and get plastered,
with the first drink of the night being accompanied by the ritualistic
Philo toast, which goes "Hold fast to the spirit of youth; let years to
come do what they may."  Why did we do all this stuff? Largely because its
fun and a good excuse to hang out with friends.

By the way, I see nothing wrong with Eric Raymond's pseudoreligion or
anything like it so long as it amuses the participants and no one
takes it seriously. I have on occassion identified myself to annoying
door-ringing proselytizers as a Bokononist or a Pope of the Church of the
Subgenius. However, I know better than to think that I "really" believe
in these fake religions. In fact, the Church of the Subgenius is a
self-conscious jokes perpetrated by one of the more rabid antireligionists
around. Now, given the FAQ that Eric posted, I see little point to his
"religious" affiliations given that he doesn't seem to actually take
them seriously and can engage in nudity with friends, orgies, and anything
else he likes without religious trappings. However, so long as he
understands that the religion is "fake" (I know he won't put it that
way, and might even deny it, but thats what he seems to be really implying)
and it amuses him I see nothing wrong with it. The only danger is to
the people who become what Subgenii call "Bobbies", meaning people who don't
get the joke and take things seriously.

