Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 18:57:28 WET
From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: META: Extropianism lost in the noise

   I have been on this list almost since the beginning and as the number
of readers has grown I have noticed a drop in quality. I have been running
statistics on this list for the past month and a half. Here are roughly
the results:

   Politics:  %40 of bandwidth
   Religion:  %40 of bandwidth
   Misc(humor, flames, news): %5
   Rest (science, philosophy, econ): %15


  This is disturbing. It used to be that a "rebel" new user would appear
on the list about once a month and would then be assimilated (Tim May
(BioHarvest), Sasha Chislenko (Anarcho-Syndicalism), etc). The level
of new rebels back then wasn't enough to overwhelm the responsible
posters who tried to respond reasonably and clarify extropian
positions. 

  Now the list is undergoing a change that occurs all too often on
usenet. As readership grows, more and more dissidents will appear tying up
the bandwidth of the list and wasting discussion time on the basics. The
typical usenet solution is to moderate (which often works wonderfully,
e.g. sci.virtual-worlds, sci.physics.moderated, sci.nanotech). I would hope
that instead of going to moderation or censorship, users of this list
who have differing opinions on "the basics" such as religion or politics
would restrain themselves. Discussion of the basics generates enormous
flamewars and volume which eclipses the other areas of
extropianism. As a result, many people simply unsubscribe rather than
reread arguments which are neverending on talk.politics.misc.

  Contrary to a point Tim May proposed a few months ago (that we have
simply ran out of things to talk about), I assert that the numerous
political and religious posts are simply overshadowing creative thought
on the list forcing people to respond to attacks rather than write
creative posts. To assume that we are finished discussing nanotech,
cryonics, uploading, crypto-privacy, etc is almost laughable.
These technologies aren't practical yet and the social effects
are incredibly large.

  Many religious and cultural memes "protect" themselves by imposing
a meta-meme preventing critical analysis. I don't think extropians
would find such meta-memes likable, but I don't know what else to
propose.  Extropians are a _tiny_, emphasis on TINY, minority of the
population. One reason for keeping this list private is to keep our
voices from being averaged out by the background noise. As this list gets
bigger and bigger, noise will surge creating chaos and causing many
valuable extropians to resign from ear-ache. As of this post, I am
calling for self-censorship of religious memes, and non-extropian politics.
I predict that if extropians do not restrain themselves, this list
is headed for moderation or splintering.

New users should read extropian literature, and then ask _specific_
questions on that literature rather than hit-and-run tactics
(e.g. Lance K Chun) like "you exploitive capitalists make me sick with your 
destruction of Gaia and third world people!"

 
  I leave you all with a quote from David's Sling

 "In the Information Age, the first step to sanity is FILTERING. Filter
the information; extract the knowledge."

  Since the sci-fi software in David's Sling isn't available yet, I propose
that we filter by censorship, either self-censorship, or moderation.
Which is more preferable?




