Date: Fri, 12 Mar 93 17:13:06 MST
From: hammar@cs.unm.edu
Subject: Meta: Cargo Cult censure? reply

    Having entered and out-grown a strongly anti-religious atheism myself,
I understand why some of the list members feel so strongly about flying 
yogis. This is not by itself an adequate reason to censor discussion
of such subjects on the list.
    There is the additional problem of signal-to-noise ratio on the list.
This is a better reason to censor such a discussion, but it must be 
applied to BOTH sides equally. If bringing up flying yogis is inappropriate,
so is replying to a flying yogi post, AND so is including material opposed
to airborne bears in a post under the same rules as the yogis must follow.
Allowing only one side of a discussion, or permitting one side to include
gratutious digs at the other in unrelated posts while the other side is 
silenced is clearly wrong.
    Religious questions like reincarnation are somewhat different. If 
Extropianism can't handle them, none of us will live long eanough to see
the basic change in humanity that would be needed to allow Extropian memes
to become even a politically important minority, much less a working 
society. The majority of people _want_ a god (or acceptable substitute for
one, like the godlike aliens so poplar with UFO fans.)
    The question of reincarnation (and similar questions about going to
heaven) can be broken up into 3 parts.
      A). Memetic: Why Extropianism is so good that you will want to 
give up reincarnation to be frozen, uploaded and whatever else we come up 
with. This is clearly a valid topic for our list. It will probably be discussed
more often without any referance to any religious topic than in a religious
context.
        1). This is positive arguements, not god-bashing. If Extropianism
gives eanough real, current benefit to someone to replace the benefit of
believing in reincarnation, it will be adopted.
        2). Arguements against reincarnation are _not_ covered under this
unless they are brand new and so potent as memes they will sweep the world
against opposition from the established memes.
      B). Practical: How do reincarnation and cryonics affect each other? Do
they absolutely conflict, can they be merged, or what? A merging strategy is
far more likely to be effective than a staight opposition strategy in 
spreading the memes throughout the general public. A percieved attack is 
always met with a defencive reaction, increasing the difficulty of gaining
converts.
      C). Not related to Extropianism: The question of whether or not
reincarnation exists may fascinate you, but it has nothing to do with this
list. Get a news feed and take your discussion to soc.religion.eastern, 
sci.skeptic, alt.discordia, alt.flame, alt.out-of-body, alt.pagan,
alt.paranormal, alt.philosophy.objectivism, talk.philosophy.misc, 
talk.religion.newage or anywhere else more closely related to reincarnation
than this list is.
        1). Overuse of the list make it far less valuable. It takes
time to go through all this mail I recieve, just to find the stuff I
need to read in the morning from my boss and others who I need to give
immediate attention to. Going through everything on the list when I have 
the time already takes too much of my time. If it gets much worse I will be 
seriously considering dropping off the list. (And I doubt I am the only one 
who will.)
        2). The same tired old arguements against reincarnation aren't
going to work without a good replacement anyway, so please don't waste
your time and mine posting them. The same goes for the arguements for
reincarnation. Noone on this list is going to change sides because you
chanted "falsifiability" or "hypnotic regression proves" at them. 

    To sum up: yes to censuring both sides of totally unrelated topics 
like flying yogis and the existance of god or reincarnation, definitely no
to stopping discussion of these topics as they relate to Extrpianism or an
Extropian sub-theme.

Neil Hammar

