Eric writes:

> Therefore, I submit for Mr. May's consideration
> a sort of non-credo --- a list of things I do *not* believe in.

This is interesting. On the one hand, I don't believe in anything
whatsoever. On the other hand, I can offer a couple of anecdotes
in evidence that counters Eric's non-beliefs.

None of the below is intended as a flame against Eric. It seems obvious
to me that Eric is attempting to qualify his neopagan beliefs to make
them more acceptable to the "hyperrationalists" on this list. Imho he
erred in asking for Tim May's "judgement". If he gains any *personal
power* from his activities then more power to him.

> I do not believe in pyramid power, crystal energy, Atlantis, astrology,
> flying yogis, or psychic TV jamming.

A good friend saw a TV show in which people used electron microscopes
to examine razor blades which had been stuck into a pyramid when not in
use. There was supposedly no difference between the wear on these blades
and a set of control blades not stuck in pyramids. My friend also claims
that some years previously he got by on 2-3 blades for a year by sticking
them in pyramids. Is it possible that some things work by believing in
them?

I can't comment on the others...

>    (These are the sorts of things I've called "vulgar superstitions" in the
> past, and I will continue to do so.)

Here Eric admits to neophobia. Whatever may happen in the future, Eric
will continue to call these "vulgar superstitions". Astrology helps
tremendously to keep the company I work for afloat. If Eric would
relax a bit he might realize the tremendous *money-making potential*
such superstitions have :)

> I do not believe in the existence of disembodied spirits or what William
> James called an "objective correlative" of godhood.

Not sure about the latter, I haven't read William James. A secretary
where I used to work claimed to see her dead husband walking out of
her house. She seemed generally conservative, almost bible-belt type
of person. She did talk at length, though, about numerous poltergeist
activities in her house (exploding glassware, etc).

>     (I routinely invoke gods and goddesses; however, I regard them as
> essentially *intersubjective* phenomena, albeit one with powerful
> objective consequences in the behavior of humans.)

Here I respectfully suggest that Eric's rituals might have more power
if he DOES regard these gods and goddesses as objectivly real. One
can always philosophise afterwards...

[on psychic abilities]:
>     (Though I concede that some of these things may be real; in particular,
> I have had some experiences that were damn difficult to explain other
> than by either an utterly implausible degree of coincidence or telepathy.)

I haven't had any, (except for lots of "synchronicities" when I was
actively practicing rituals and studying magick), but several people
I know and consider to be quite sane in day-to-day life have. One 
friend experienced repeated and uncontrolled "astral projection". My
sister used to hear tomorrow's news on the radio. One of my SO's
relatives had a premonition of a fire in the house. Another friend,
when picking a card at random from a Tarot deck to represent himself,
picked the same card 3 times in a row. It scared him a great deal: he
didn't study the cards for months afterward. The point is, things which
one might call "psychic phenomena" have happened to a lot of people.
RA Wilson has written lots on such things, but I guess he (like FM-2030)
is yet another writer on the extropian reading list who isn't quite
extropian himself (I'll bet there's a lot of them).

When something isn't reproducible in laboratory conditions, one can
conclude:
	a)	it doesn't have an objective existence, or
	b)	it's not reproducible in laboratory conditions

Am I alone in thinking that 'b' is the more rational choice?

