I'd more or less like to see the non-extropian topics go away.

I must note that I am not a closed minded individual. I freely discuss many
topics, including mysticism. I don't want to see all discussion of
non-extropian correct positions disappear from the face of the earth.

However, I intended this list, when I started it, to be a "safe haven". There
are a dozen megabytes of flame wars between statists and anti-statists,
between atheists and theists, etc, going by on Usenet every day. If you
want to find people who want to discuss whether or not you are capable of
altering Dan Rather's blink rate by psychic means or whether Krishna
is a real entity, there are loads of places to do that -- indeed, there
are loads of places to do that where you will likely run in to many
of the same players you see here on Extropians. However, quite frankly,
I don't like seeing a number of these individuals here. This is a place
for discussing EXTROPIANISM. Now, were we all sitting inside a barn
in Waco, Texas and only allowed to get our news of the world from Max More
and the Holy Cannon of Extropian Thought, I'd wonder whether or not
we were risking going off our rockers due to lack of context. However,
I have no trouble getting context. I get plenty of context. I hear the
goddamn socialists on National Socialist er, Public, Radio every night
telling me how much better a place the world will be after we've put
price controls on health care. I don't need people to tell me that
there are people out there who believe in gods -- hell, I always seem
to end up dating them.

This is not to say that I would like to see folks like Pandit Singh
kicked off the list. I would just appreciate it if they understood that
their idea set was not considered to be the local norm and if they
understood that this is not a place to get your jollies by telling us
without any evidence or facts about how Krishna can divide himself
into a million parts and have sex with the cowherds at the side of the
river. I'm an openminded person but that doesn't mean that I don't have
firm beliefs. When someone gives me a shred of evidence I will likely
change my opinion, but right now I feel very confident in saying that
Mr. Singh is completely wrong, that iDeAlOrDer or however he capitalizes
it is either pulling our legs or off his rocker, etc. I see nothing
wrong with these people having their opinions and expressing them, but
I'd prefer that they didn't express them on our mailing list. As with
Tim, I'm a little sick of seeing people tell me I'm closed minded
because I won't believe that if you only sit in a large circle and
chant long enough you can get 50 story buildings to sprout legs
and start tapdancing, and I'm also a little sick of such individuals
increasingly dominating our list.

I am not, as Owen accused us, a closed minded fanatic. I'm a person
with a very open minded view of the world -- but I also am rather
firmly in the camp of only accepting that for which there is evidence.
There are, as we all know, an infinite number of non-falsifyable notions.
If I went up to you tomorrow and told you that an undetectable invisible,
silent and weightless rabbit was following your mother at all times,
and you told me "thats silly", you would not be closed minded -- you'd merely
be rational.

Now, Eric Drexler has noted that far-edge ideas attract far edge people,
many of whom are "loonies" and assume that just because you are
non-mainstream you must somehow be their friend. Many of our ideas ARE
seemingly rather nutty. After all, most people don't go around talking
about how they are going to have their body frozen when they deanimate
and why the government is a bad idea. This makes people who believe
in reincarnation or the evils of wearing shoes think that we are fellow
travelers, even though we are not.

I'll point out that caution is always in order. We claim to be scientific, but
then again so do Marxists. Allowing ourselves to slip into self delusion
about how much we do and don't know will leave us just as badly off
as the mystics. Questioning our own beliefs is always in order, as is
exposing ourselves to different ideas so as to assure that we never become
dogmatic or closed minded.

However, I will repeat one more time, that exposure does not have to happen
here -- and although I will not flame at the promulgators of pseudoscience
and new-age yogic intenstinal purging, I do not see any reason that we need
to accept them in what is essentially our own private party.

Perry

