It has been argued that racism played a large part in the initial
(U.S.) prohibition of drugs.  While this is obviously something that
anti-prohibitionists would *like* to believe, I find it plausible,
at least in respect to the classic cases of opium, marijuana, and
cocaine.  The modern WoD looks like simple puritanism -- who was it
that described puritanism as "the fear that someone, somewhere, is
having fun"?.  The peak of the drug frenzy coincided with that of
the porn panic.  Really, Reagan's WoD is nothing new; it's happened
at least once a decade since WW2.  And it gets worse each time
around, because people *really believe* that there is a problem, and
that fascism will solve it, and that if it doesn't then we obviously
need more of it.

To a first approximation, it seems to happen during economic
expansions.  It looks like people have a deep-seated need for
"decisive government action" on one front or another.

