Subject: PHIL META a challenge to be rational?

> 
> Being rational is a religion and denies history. Tell me, how do
> we know what history really was without using reason? Without reason,
> who is to say what is and is not true? Without reason, the only
> thing that keeps you from getting killed by the next bus that crosses
> your path is blind luck.
> 
> Reason is not crap. Personally, I have no care about convincing you of
> this -- as Ayn Rand has pointed out, the denial of reason is in some
> sense its own punishment as those who deny reason in the end die because
> of it.

I do not beleive that in decrying 'hyperrationalism' he was advocating the
abstinence from logical thought that you seem to think. It is certainly easy
to mock someone who does not believe in the use of logic, but we all no that
straw men burn well.
	In attacking 'hyperrationalism' I believe that he was attacking, not
logic, but the (often connected) beliefs that simple logic can solve all 
problems (and logic can't help you choose your axioms, by definition), and
the strong support of the scientific orthodoxy. Both of these are subtle
fallacies, and against the 'pancritical rationalism' advocated by some on
this list and many extropians (K. Eric Drexler mentions it in Nanosystems,
for example). The scientific orthodoxy is generally a reliable source of
information because of its use of the scientific method, a system that sits
well with the critical mind set. When someone believes something because it
is the conventional scientific view, but they have no particular data to
draw on, they are possibly guilty of just as great a fallacy as the person
who believes in something withoout eveidence. Science has been wrong before.
And if you believe all problems can be solved by logic, you have a very dim
understanding of the limits of logic, and limits there are, of differing kinds
well known to philosophers and computer scientists.

					Dave Cake

